8:00">February 13, 2026</time></div>
		<h1 class=3D"wp-block-post-title">You Don=E2=80=99t Write the Code. You D=
on=E2=80=99t Read the Code Either.</h1>
	</div>
=09

	<div class=3D"entry-content wp-block-post-content has-global-padding is-la=
yout-constrained wp-block-post-content-is-layout-constrained">
<p>A few months ago, I walked into Justin McCarthy=E2=80=99s office at Stro=
ngDM and noticed something wrong with his colleague Jay=E2=80=99s monitor.<=
/p>



<p>He had a Google Spreadsheet open. Columns, rows, formatting =E2=80=93 it=
 looked exactly like Sheets. Except the URL bar said localhost.</p>



<p>That could not be so.</p>



<p>Gsuite was not alone. Slack was there, Jira, Okta=E2=80=A6 all running l=
ocally? A digital twin of the entire enterprise SaaS universe, there on tha=
t desktop, faithful enough that the Python client libraries couldn=E2=80=99=
t tell the difference. Jay confirmed that it was in fact what it looked lik=
e; he built it himself. It took a couple of weeks. He used their <a href=3D=
"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Feb/7/software-factory/">Dark Factory</a>.<=
/p>



<p>The essence of what I learned from them could fit on a notecard.</p>



<hr class=3D"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity">



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Standing on One Foot</h2>



<p>I=E2=80=99ve been having quiet conversations with Justin McCarthy, CTO o=
f StrongDM, for months now. I wrote about the first one =E2=80=93 a dinner =
at Piccino where he told me about <a href=3D"https://www.danshapiro.com/blo=
g/2025/10/slot-machine-development/">slot machine development</a>, and I wa=
lked away patting myself on the back thinking I understood a little bit of =
the future now. Since then, every few weeks he=E2=80=99d tell me something =
new, and every few weeks I=E2=80=99d nod politely while privately thinking =
he was=E2=80=A6 let=E2=80=99s say optimistic. Exaggerating would be rude. N=
obody=E2=80=99s actually doing that.</p>



<p>This week he got on stage and showed it to the world. I was in the room.=
 He was not exaggerating.</p>



<p>The essence of what Justin=E2=80=99s team learned fits on an index card.=
</p>



<p><strong>First</strong>, you recognize that, if you want to move quickly,=
 you=E2=80=99re not the person best qualified to be writing code anymore. T=
he AI writes the code.</p>



<p><strong>Second,</strong> you recognize that if you=E2=80=99re not writin=
g the code, and you=E2=80=99re still reviewing every pull request, <em>you<=
/em> are the bottleneck. So you have to stop reading the code, too.</p>



<p><strong>Third</strong>, you realize this creates an enormous pile of ter=
rifying problems. If nobody=E2=80=99s writing code, who understands it? If =
nobody=E2=80=99s reading the code, how do you know it works? How do you kno=
w it=E2=80=99s getting better instead of worse?</p>



<p><strong>Finally</strong> =E2=80=93 and this is the part that takes a min=
ute to land =E2=80=93 you realize that solving those problems is your actua=
l job now.</p>



<p>That=E2=80=99s it. That=E2=80=99s the whole thing. That=E2=80=99s how yo=
u build a software factory. Everything else is commentary. You can stop rea=
ding and go figure it out.</p>



<hr class=3D"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity">



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">You Can=E2=80=99t Look Inside</h2>



<p>So you don=E2=80=99t read the code. You built something, it=E2=80=99s in=
side a sealed box, and you have no idea what=E2=80=99s going on in there.</=
p>



<p>It seems strange, but we=E2=80=99ve been here before.</p>



<p>When I was 10 years old, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonar=
d_Shapiro">my dad</a>, a professor of computer science, explained this whil=
e carving a roasted chicken at the dinner table.</p>



<p>=E2=80=9CYou see, everyone used to write the code by hand. But now there=
 are these =E2=80=98high level languages=E2=80=99. People say it=E2=80=99s =
cheating to use them. But getting things done is <em>so much faster</em>.=
=E2=80=9D</p>



<p>People would complain that you had to hand-optimize the assembler. They =
would be horrified that you=E2=80=99d ship assembly code you=E2=80=99ve nev=
er read.</p>



<p>History rhymes.</p>



<hr class=3D"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity">



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Monday, Then Tuesday</h2>



<p>The Monday before their release, they built CXDB. It=E2=80=99s a special=
-purpose observability layer that watches every interaction in their system=
.</p>



<p>On Tuesday, they built Healer.</p>



<p>Healer watches CXDB, develops opinions about whether agent behaviors loo=
k right, and clusters similar problems into diagnoses. Those diagnoses beco=
me investigations =E2=80=93 and the investigations are themselves agents. A=
n agent wakes up, looks at the cluster of bad behavior, finds the relevant =
code and prompts and data, and writes a prescription. The prescription gets=
 applied. The bug gets fixed.</p>



<p>No human filed the bug report. No human triaged it. No human wrote the f=
ix.</p>



<p>This is what I found most interesting about their building process. Last=
 year, =E2=80=9Clet=E2=80=99s spend our time on the tools before we start t=
he product=E2=80=9D was foolish. This year, it=E2=80=99s more like the quot=
e attributed to Abraham Lincoln: =E2=80=9CGive me six hours to chop down a =
tree, and I will spend the first four sharpening my axe.=E2=80=9D</p>



<hr class=3D"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity">



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">The Digital Twin Universe</h2>



<p>Back to GSheets on localhost.</p>



<p>StrongDM is an enterprise security company. They build access management=
 for complex enterprises. In their world, =E2=80=98security issue=E2=80=99 =
is a euphemism for =E2=80=98lawsuit=E2=80=99.</p>



<p>Once you=E2=80=99ve realized you don=E2=80=99t write the code, and you d=
on=E2=80=99t read the code, the biggest problem is quality. And the best so=
lution to quality is testing =E2=80=93 in the most realistic environment yo=
u can.</p>



<p>You see where this is going.</p>



<p>As Justin put it: =E2=80=9CA year ago, if an eager new hire had walked i=
nto my office and suggested =E2=80=98let=E2=80=99s just clone GSuite and Sa=
lesforce and Okta and every SaaS application our product integrates with,=
=E2=80=99 I would have said: your enthusiasm is really welcome. Get back to=
 work. Project not approved.=E2=80=9D</p>



<p>=E2=80=9CAnd I would have been wrong. Jay built the whole suite in a cou=
ple of weeks. The replicas are faithful enough for every externally observa=
ble behavior the agents need =E2=80=93 not a full reimplementation of Googl=
e Sheets, but close enough that the agents can=E2=80=99t tell the differenc=
e.=E2=80=9D</p>



<hr class=3D"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity">



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Why Am I Doing This?</h2>



<p>Justin=E2=80=99s team has a mantra. Whenever anyone finds themselves doi=
ng something manually =E2=80=93 reviewing logs, checking output, validating=
 behavior =E2=80=93 they stop and ask one question:</p>



<p><em>Why am I doing this?</em></p>



<p>If you=E2=80=99re looking at a log file and something doesn=E2=80=99t lo=
ok right, and you can articulate <em>why</em> it doesn=E2=80=99t look right=
, you=E2=80=99ve just described a validation rule. And if you can describe =
it, you can automate it. So stop looking at log files. Get yourself out of =
the job of looking.</p>



<p>This is the discipline that separates a team that uses AI from a softwar=
e factory. It=E2=80=99s relentless, almost uncomfortable. But it=E2=80=99s =
also the most concrete thing you can take away from Justin=E2=80=99s work: =
the next time you find yourself doing something, ask how you can stop.</p>



<hr class=3D"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity">



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">What Next?</h2>



<p>Justin=E2=80=99s team is three people. A CTO, a senior software engineer=
ing manager, and a new hire less than a year out of school. That team built=
 it all =E2=80=93 the factory, the features, the digital twin universe. Now=
 Navan, the new guy, is leading bootcamps where dozens of StrongDM senior d=
evelopers learn the new way software is built.</p>



<p>StrongDM=E2=80=99s experience is what happens when building software bec=
omes a means instead of an end. A compiler =E2=80=93 not one that turns BAS=
IC into machine code, but one that turns problems into solutions. Our job i=
s to keep the ideas flowing, and the factory running.</p>



<p>It happened to software first. I don=E2=80=99t think it=E2=80=99s going =
to stop there.</p>



<p>If you=E2=80=99d like to build something with a dark factory yourself, y=
ou should. There are a few options. You can read more of the team=E2=80=99s=
 work at <a href=3D"https://factory.strongdm.ai/">https://factory.strongdm.=
ai/</a>. You can try out a real implementation of their software factory at=
 <a href=3D"https://github.com/danshapiro/kilroy">https://github.com/dansha=
piro/kilroy</a>. Or =E2=80=93 meat for another blog post =E2=80=93 you can =
take a trip to the incredible, Mad-Max infused magic of <a href=3D"https://=
steve-yegge.medium.com/welcome-to-gas-town-4f25ee16dd04">Steve Yegge=E2=80=
=99s Gas Town</a>.</p>



<p> Time to sharpen our axes.</p>



<p></p>


